{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Priceville homes — especially newer construction — have inadequate downspouts. Builders often install the minimum required by code: standard 2x3 inch downspouts that can't handle Morgan County's heavy rainfall. During intense storms, these undersized downspouts back up, causing gutters to overflow.</p>

<p>Older Priceville homes have different issues. Original downspouts may have been adequate decades ago but are now damaged, corroded, or disconnected. Extensions that once moved water away from the foundation have been damaged by mowers, kicked aside, or simply removed.</p>

<p>The result is water pooling near foundations throughout Priceville. On our area's clay soil, that pooled water doesn't absorb — it spreads and persists. Over time, this leads to foundation cracks, basement moisture, and expensive repairs.</p>

<p>We're just 8 miles away in Hartselle and can quickly assess your Priceville property. We'll tell you exactly what you need — no more, no less.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and our area's rainfall. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle heavy storms without backing up.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows to corners and low points in your gutter system.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For properties with challenging drainage, underground drains route water 10+ feet away.</p>
{% endset %}

